¶

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To beat; give a thump or blow.

{{smaller block|As though my heart-strings had been cracked I wept and sighed, and ''thumped'' and ''thumped'', and raved and randed and railed.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, iv. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|As he approached the stream, his heart began to ''thump.''
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 448.}}}}

'''thump''' (thump), ''n.'' [< ''thump'', ''v.''] A heavy blow, or the sound made by such a blow; a blow with a club, the fist, or anything that gives a thick, heavy sound; a bang: as, to give one a ''thump''.

{{smaller block|Long hair . . . is, in peace, an ornament; in war, a strong helmet; it blunts the edge of a sword, and deads the leaden ''thump'' of a bullet.
{{right|''Dekker'', Gull's Hornbook, p. 89.}}}}

{{smaller block|The watchman's ''thump'' at midnight startles us in our beds as much as the breaking in of a thief.
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 251.}}}}

'''thumper''' (thum′pėr), ''n.'' [< ''thump'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' One who or that which thumps.—'''2.''' A thing or a person that is impressive by reason of hugeness or greatness; an unusually big fish, lie, etc.; a whopper. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|He cherished his friend, and he relished a bumper;
Yet one fault he had, and that one was a ''thumper.''
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Retaliation.}}}}

'''thumping''' (thum′ping), ''p. a.'' [Ppr. of ''thump''.] Unusually large or heavy; big. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|Let us console that martyr, I say, with ''thumping'' damages; and as for the woman—the guilty wretch! let us lead her out and stone her.
{{right|''Thackeray.''}}}}

